body:
The review of task:2414798176c466e4 found `crate::failure::...` (previously `crate::diagnostics::...`) in signatures and bodies throughout the split. This is the tool's doing: `extract::rust.moved_reference` always returns `full_path(target, name)`, so step 22 rewrote 640 symbols to fully qualified paths. That is correct but makes signatures long and cuts off outline lines. The worker then has to add `use` lines and shorten the bodies by hand.

When `extract` rewrites a moved reference in a staying module, prefer a `use` line and the short name when the name is free in that module. Fall back to the full path when the short name would clash with a local item, another import, or a name the module uses. Add one import per distinct item, and do not leave an unused import.

Add unit tests in `extract::rust::tests`:

- several signatures in one module using a moved type get one `use crate::failure::Failure;` and short names;
- a module with its own `Failure` keeps the full path for the moved one;
- a module with an existing import of the moved item does not get a duplicate;
- `check --warnings` stays clean in each case.

Run `check --warnings`, `rust-unit`, `rust-acceptance` and `spacing_parity`.

Out of scope: a general `solace tidy` command and a crate-root re-export. A re-export is a fine fallback if the worker finds it smaller, but the behavior to test is the readable code, not the mechanism.
